# 25. Protein Stability


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_077.jpg)


@right


- Fold stability
- Disulfide bonds
- Protein engineering
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Stability


@left


- What forces help a protein retain its folded structure under changing conditions?
- How can mutations, disulfide bonds, ligands, or formulation improve stability?
- Why might a more stable protein lose activity or become harder to produce?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_077.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Stability


@left


- Stability reflects many interactions, including hydrophobic packing, hydrogen bonds, salt bridges, and disulfide bonds.
- Protein engineering can redesign selected residues or introduce a disulfide bond to favor a stable fold.
- Buffers, salts, compatible additives, and ligands can stabilize some proteins during storage or use.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_077.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Stability - Design tradeoffs


@left


- Proline can restrict backbone motion; arginine can add favorable interactions, but effects depend on position and context.
- A mutation that stabilizes one structure may reduce flexibility needed for binding or catalysis.
- Compare stability, activity, solubility, and yield; there is no single residue change that improves every protein.


@right


![Protein stability engineering examples from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Stability - Identify routes to instability


@left


- Heat, pH, solvent, or mutation can shift the balance between folded and unfolded states.
- Flexible loops, exposed hydrophobic patches, weak interfaces, and reactive residues can contribute to instability.
- A change that helps one protein can reduce another protein's solubility or activity.


@right


![Protein stability example showing an engineered disulfide bond](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Stability - Engineer the fold


@left


- A new disulfide bond can restrain a flexible region when geometry and redox conditions support it.
- Core packing, hydrogen bonds, surface charge, helix capping, and loop length can be tuned through sequence changes.
- Ligands, cofactors, or binding partners can stabilize some proteins by favoring a structured state.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_077.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Stability - Test stability without losing function


@left


- Compare melting behavior, aggregation, soluble yield, storage lifetime, and retained activity across conditions.
- Thermal resistance, chemical stability, and shelf life are related but distinct properties.
- A useful design preserves folding, binding, catalytic activity, and manufacturability.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_077.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to fold stability](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_076.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 26. Binding Sites and Scaffolds


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@right


- Binding surface
- Scaffold design
- Molecular recognition
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Binding Sites and Scaffolds


@left


- How do the shape and chemistry of a binding site determine recognition or enzyme activity?
- What role does the protein scaffold play around a functional site?
- Why can changing a site affect specificity, catalytic rate, and stability at the same time?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Binding Sites and Scaffolds


@left


- A binding site positions a ligand through complementary shape, charge, hydrogen bonds, and hydrophobic contacts.
- The scaffold holds the site in a folded structure and can affect access, flexibility, and stability.
- A design that improves one interaction can disrupt folding or activity elsewhere; measure the whole protein.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Binding Sites and Scaffolds - Engineering


@left


- Structural models and molecular simulations can suggest candidate mutations but cannot guarantee performance in an assay.
- Smaller scaffolds can be easier to produce or redesign, but may lose stabilizing contacts or useful context.
- Larger scaffolds can support extra functions but may increase folding, delivery, or manufacturing challenges.


@right


![Protein domain assembly from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Binding Sites and Scaffolds - Redesign a binding site


@left


- A binding site includes residues that contact a ligand and residues that position or stabilize those contacts.
- Changing charge, shape, flexibility, or hydrogen-bond partners can alter affinity and selectivity.
- A mutation can improve one interaction while weakening stability or binding to another partner.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Binding Sites and Scaffolds - Use a scaffold as a framework


@left


- A scaffold provides a stable fold that displays a binding surface, catalytic residues, or modular domains.
- Small scaffolds can be easier to express and redesign; larger scaffolds can support multiple functions or complex interfaces.
- Scaffold size alone does not predict performance; folding, dynamics, expression, and context matter.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Binding Sites and Scaffolds - Test the engineered protein


@left


- Measure binding affinity and selectivity against related targets rather than testing only one positive sample.
- Use structural models to propose changes, then test purified variants in biochemical and cell-based assays.
- Check aggregation, solubility, expression yield, and stability alongside the intended effect.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to binding surface](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 27. Protein Directed Evolution


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to mutation](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_082.png)


@bottom-left


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)


@right


- Mutation
- Selection
- Improved function
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Directed Evolution


@left


- How do mutation, library construction, screening, and selection mimic parts of natural evolution?
- What do error-prone PCR and DNA shuffling contribute to a protein library?
- Why can a small library miss useful variants?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mutation](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_082.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Directed Evolution


@left


- Researchers generate protein variants, test a chosen property, retain better performers, and repeat the cycle.
- Error-prone PCR introduces mutations during copying; DNA shuffling recombines related gene fragments.
- For 100 residues, changing one position to any of the other 19 amino acids gives 1,900 possible single substitutions.


@top-right


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mutation](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_078.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Directed Evolution - Selection


@left


- A screen measures variant performance; a selection links survival or reproduction to the desired function.
- Library design and assay quality constrain which improvements can be found.
- A selected variant needs retesting and characterization outside the selection conditions.


@right


![DNA shuffling for protein evolution from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_082.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Directed Evolution - Run a directed-evolution cycle


@left


- Create gene variants, express the protein library, and select or screen for the desired phenotype.
- Recover improved variants, sequence them, and repeat with adjusted mutation and selection conditions.
- Evolution depends on a measurable phenotype linked to each variant's genotype.


@right


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Directed Evolution - Think about library coverage


@left


- A protein of 100 residues has 100 positions and 19 alternative amino acids per position for a single substitution.
- That gives 100 x 19 = 1,900 possible single-amino-acid substitutions before considering combinations.
- A practical library samples only a fraction of sequence space, so design and screening capacity matter.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mutation](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_082.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Directed Evolution - Separate selection from screening


@left


- Selection enriches variants that survive or bind under a condition; screening measures individual variants.
- Selection pressure should match the desired trait, such as activity at higher temperature or binding to a target.
- Retest top variants under relevant conditions and check that gains are not measurement artifacts.


@top-right


![Student-created diagram of mutation, selection, and recovery in directed evolution.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_directed-evolution-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mutation](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_078.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 28. Recombinant Protein Domains


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@right


- Protein domains
- Modular function
- New combinations
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Recombinant Protein Domains


@left


- How can protein domains be combined to create a new or improved function?
- How does DNA shuffling differ from changing one selected residue?
- What should be tested after two domains are joined in a recombinant protein?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Recombinant Protein Domains


@left


- Domains are folded regions that can contribute binding, catalysis, localization, or structural support.
- Recombinant designs can join domains from different proteins; DNA shuffling can recombine related sequences into new variants.
- Linkers, domain order, folding, activity, and stability all affect whether the combined protein works.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Recombinant Protein Domains - Evaluation


@left


- Screen combinations for the intended function instead of assuming that useful domains remain independent after fusion.
- Compare the fusion with each parent protein and appropriate controls.
- Therapeutic domain products need product-specific evidence for activity, safety, delivery, and manufacturing quality.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Recombinant Protein Domains - Combine protein domains


@left


- Domains are folded units that can contribute binding, catalysis, localization, or regulation.
- Domain boundaries and linkers affect folding, orientation, and communication between parts.
- Test a domain fusion for expression, solubility, activity, and unintended interactions.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Recombinant Protein Domains - Build libraries by recombination


@left


- DNA shuffling fragments related genes and reassembles them to combine sequence segments from different parents.
- Combinatorial libraries vary domain order, linker length, or residue choices across many constructs.
- Sequence the best-performing combinations to identify features that support the phenotype.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Recombinant Protein Domains - Select useful modular designs


@left


- A functional module can enter a compatible scaffold if interfaces and geometry are preserved.
- Zinc-finger arrays illustrate how repeated DNA-binding domains can be assembled to recognize longer target sequences.
- Test binding specificity and off-target effects because modular assembly does not guarantee unique recognition.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_083.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein domains](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_080.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 29. De Novo Protein Design


@top-left


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@right


- Designed sequences
- Target fold
- Function testing
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: De Novo Protein Design


@left


- How is designing a new protein from scratch different from modifying a natural protein?
- How do computational design and experimental testing inform each other?
- Which measurements are needed to show that a designed protein folds and performs its intended function?


@top-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: De Novo Protein Design


@left


- De novo design starts with a target structure or function and searches for sequences that could realize it.
- Computational models propose candidates; experiments test expression, folding, structure, and activity.
- A design is not validated by a model alone: independent measurements must support the intended properties.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: De Novo Protein Design - Design cycle


@left


- Design, build, test, and redesign form an iterative cycle.
- Successful folding is one requirement; binding, catalysis, selectivity, or stability may each need separate assays.
- Designed proteins could support research, therapeutics, materials, or synthetic biology when the intended function is demonstrated.


@top-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: De Novo Protein Design - Design a protein from first principles


@left


- Choose a target function, desired geometry, and structural constraints before selecting a sequence.
- Computational design proposes sequences likely to adopt a target fold or assemble into a chosen structure.
- Synthesize, express, purify, and test designed sequences; use results to guide another round.


@right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: De Novo Protein Design - Validate a de novo design


@left


- Check whether the protein expresses, folds, and forms its intended oligomer or assembly.
- Use biophysical methods to test stability and structure, then measure intended binding or activity directly.
- An in-silico design may fail because of aggregation, competing conformations, or cellular conditions.


@top-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: De Novo Protein Design - Distinguish design from prediction


@left


- Structure prediction estimates a fold for a given sequence; de novo design seeks a sequence for a desired structure or function.
- Experimental structures can test whether a designed sequence adopted its intended fold.
- Successful designs can provide new binders, sensors, cages, or enzymes, each requiring functional evidence.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to designed sequences](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_084.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created examples of designed protein folds.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_de-novo-protein-folds.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 30. Four-Base Codons and Expanded Genetic Codes


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to expanded codons](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_086.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to expanded codons](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_087.jpg)


@right


- Expanded codons
- Noncanonical amino acids
- Site-specific chemistry
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Expanded Genetic Codes


@left


- How can engineered tRNA and aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase pairs add a noncanonical amino acid to a protein?
- Why might a four-base codon be used, and what translation problems can arise?
- How could a new chemical group enable labeling, crosslinking, or protein purification?


@top-right


